State 
====
--It is like a variable it is used to handle the dynamic nature of 
a component or store the data

Hooks
====
--hooks are built in functions that is used to handle react features
-- all hooks need to be imported.

types
1.useState
2.useEffect

1.useState
=======
--It is a hook that is used to manage the state
it is used to store data dynamically.

import{useState} from 'react'


let [count,setCount]=useState(0); //used to store data in a component
{count}
<button onclick={()=>setCount(count+1)}>inc</button>

theory of state---> it is like a variable name
 setState- it is a function that is used to change the value of a state

 setState(2)
 useState(initial value)

 2. useEffect()
it is a hook that is used to manage side effects such as 
API calling,localstorage,settimeout,setinterval etc...
 it has 3 types

import{useEffect} from 'react';

1st type - useEffect with no dependency
it will run everytime when the state changes
so it has low efficiency 
so we will not use this
and api always asks for premium after  some limit or trails
useEffect(()=>{
      //api logic
      console.log('apicalling)
})

2nd type -useEffect with empty dependence
it will run only one time
it will run again when the page is refreshed
useEffect(()=>{
      console.log('apicalling')
},[])

3rd type -useEffect with dependency
it will run one time at first and re runs only when the dependencies changes

useEffect(()=>{
      console.log('apicalling')
},[state1,state2,state3])

each hook is an interview question in react
interview ans
used to run sideeffects such as api calling and local storage 
it has 3 types 
1st - runs every time
2nd - runs only once and when it is refereshes the page
3rd -runs  once and runs when the revelent data is changed (http  services like update,delete)
    it is best and advance  to use


